<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayburt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayburt is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, capital of Bayburt province, on the Çoruh river. It lay on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, and its large medieval castle was rebuilt under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum in the 13th century. In the 12th century it belonged to the Saltukids of Erzurum, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the early 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayburt.jpg" alt="Bayburt" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ben Bender · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayburt,_69000_Bayburt_Merkez-Bayburt,_Turkey_-_panoramio_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bayburt straddles the Çoruh river on an open and fertile plateau, on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>. The name comes from the medieval Armenian Baydbert, whose second part is most probably the Armenian loanword bert, meaning fortress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> calls the city Baiberdon and the chronicler Kedrenos Paiperte, while the Armenian historian Movses of Khoren names it Paypert and says its ancient name was Smpadapert, after Smbat I, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratuni dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bayburt was once part of Hayasa-Azzi and was afterwards settled or conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> in the 8th century BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> in the 7th century BCE. It then passed through the Persian Empire, Lesser Armenia, Pontus, Greater Armenia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, the Bagratid Armenian Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and Safavid Persia, before coming under the Ottoman Turks. An Armenian fortress stood on the site in the 1st century, and the place may be the Baiberdon fortified by the emperor Justinian. The Seljuks raided it in 1054, Theodore Gabras recaptured it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> conquered it before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1101.</p><p>In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a stronghold of the Genovese and prospered in the late 13th and early 14th century through trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. It had a mint under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a>, and from about 1243 to 1266 it was briefly held by the Georgian princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>. Marco Polo visited in the 13th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> in the 16th. Under Ottoman rule it was the centre of the Bayburt Sanjak in Erzurum Eyalet, and for a time in the early 16th century, when Erzurum was devastated, the de facto capital of the province.</p><p>A Russian army under General Paskevich captured Bayburt in 1829 and thoroughly demolished its fortifications, the farthest westward point the Russians reached in that campaign. The British geologist William Hamilton described the ruins in the 1840s, and the population grew to about 6,000 by the 1870s. On the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> about 10,000 people lived there, mostly Turkish with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bayburt is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the seat of Bayburt Province, with a population of 48,036 in 2021. Bayburt Castle stands on steep rocks north of the city; it was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratuni dynasty</a> in the 9th to 11th centuries and rebuilt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and again in the Ottoman period, and it was inhabited until its destruction in 1829. Its walls and masonry placed it among the finest castles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. The city also has historical mosques, hammams and tombs. Nearby are the Aydıntepe underground city and the Dome of Dede Korkut, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> included on its Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayburt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Saltukids held Erzurum, Tercan, <b>Bayburt</b> and Ispir, on the frontier with the Kingdom of Georgia, which defeated them several times; Izz al-Din Saltuk II was captured by the Georgians in the 1150s and ransomed.</blockquote><a href="/state/saltukids">Saltukids</a></li></ul></section>
